A correct implementation should:

- read the filename from the terminal;
- read $k$ tasks from that file;
- process time slots in chronological order;
- when the machine is free at time $s$, choose among all unscheduled tasks with start time $s$ the one with smallest finish time;
- output every scheduled task in chronological order.

One suitable algorithm is:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class MachineA {
    static class Task {
        int start, finish;
        Task(int start, int finish) {
            this.start = start;
            this.finish = finish;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner terminal = new Scanner(System.in);
        String filename = terminal.nextLine().trim();

        Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(filename));
        int k = in.nextInt();

        TreeMap<Integer, PriorityQueue<Task>> byStart = new TreeMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            int a = in.nextInt();
            int b = in.nextInt();
            Task t = new Task(a, b);
            byStart.computeIfAbsent(a, x -> new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(task -> task.finish))).add(t);
        }

        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
        int currentTime = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for (Map.Entry<Integer, PriorityQueue<Task>> entry : byStart.entrySet()) {
            int start = entry.getKey();
            if (start >= currentTime) {
                Task chosen = entry.getValue().poll();
                out.append(chosen.start).append(' ').append(chosen.finish).append('\n');
                currentTime = chosen.finish;
            }
        }

        System.out.print(out.toString());
    }
}
```

Use the standard interval scheduling greedy algorithm: sort all tasks by increasing finish time, and whenever a task starts no earlier than the finish time of the last chosen task, schedule it.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class MachineB {
    static class Task {
        int start, finish;
        Task(int start, int finish) {
            this.start = start;
            this.finish = finish;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner terminal = new Scanner(System.in);
        String filename = terminal.nextLine().trim();

        Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(filename));
        int k = in.nextInt();
        Task[] tasks = new Task[k];

        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            int a = in.nextInt();
            int b = in.nextInt();
            tasks[i] = new Task(a, b);
        }

        Arrays.sort(tasks, (x, y) -> {
            if (x.finish != y.finish) return Integer.compare(x.finish, y.finish);
            return Integer.compare(x.start, y.start);
        });

        int count = 0;
        int currentFinish = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for (Task task : tasks) {
            if (task.start >= currentFinish) {
                count++;
                currentFinish = task.finish;
            }
        }

        System.out.println(count);
    }
}
```

The running time is $O(k \log k)$ because the tasks are sorted once and then scanned once.
